#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# region_integrate

#% text
# `region_integrate(f, [x, y, ...], reg)` is the integral of `f` over the region `reg`; `region_integrate(f, element([x, y], reg))` is the same.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The integral is written as iterated integrals of the region's parametrisation: boxes directly, disks and balls in polar and spherical coordinates, triangles, simplices and (triangulated) polygons over the standard simplex, curves and surfaces with their length or area element.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Over the unit disk:

#% cell
region_integrate(x^2 + y^2, [x, y], disk())
#% output
#= (1/2)*pi

#% text
# Over a triangle:

#% cell
region_integrate(x*y, [x, y], triangle())
#% output
#= 1/24

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The volume of a ball:

#% cell
region_integrate(1, [x, y, z], ball([0, 0, 0], 2))
#% output
#= (32/3)*pi

#% text
# A line integral around a circle:

#% cell
region_integrate(1, [x, y], circle([0, 0], 3))
#% output
#= 6*pi

#% text
# An interval:

#% cell
region_integrate(x^2, element([x], interval([0, 1])))
#% output
#= 1/3

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `region_measure`, `integrate`
